WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
So Guy, Tessl have just released agent skill support.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.800
What does that mean for people who have already built skills?

3
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.960
it means that you can treat those skills

4
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.680
as actual pieces of software, and not just words that you copy around.

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.000
You can package them up, version them, publish them for people to consume.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.160
You can run some tests and evals to see if they're any good,

7
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.200
and you can own them over time and evolve them as the software

8
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.280
described, sort of evolves on its own.

9
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.800
The evals sound really interesting because I know there are tens

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.880
of thousands of skills out there, and I have no idea which ones to use.

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.520
how can evals help here?

12
00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.240
So everything that we index in the registry, we immediately assess it.

13
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.040
We evaluate it and we give it a score.

14
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.120
So right away if you're looking for, you know, two things that help you

15
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.360
manipulate browsers.

16
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.480
Say you will see some score of, you know which one might be a little bit

17
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.240
better to use.

18
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.600
And you can drill down and get the details to understand,

19
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.640
you know, what does that mean and what's right for you.

20
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.560
That sounds super useful to work out which skills are most effective

21
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:54.520
in my environment.

22
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.760
Tell us a little bit more about the two different types of evals

23
00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.360
that Tessl runs on your skills.

24
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.640
So one is called review eval and it's assessing whether a skill matches

25
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.760
the best practices.

26
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.520
That Anthropic put out for a skill.

27
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.080
Is it concise enough?

28
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.000
Is it split up into small files?

29
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.960
Does it have the right kind of activation instructions for the agent to load up?

30
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.800
That one we run on all the skills.

31
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.600
The second one is called task evaluation.

32
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.600
In that case we actually define a task that represents,

33
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.640
you know, what should this skill do?

34
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:25.800
And run an agent

35
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.840
through it with and without the skill and see how well it fares.

36
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.040
How much did the skill actually help?

37
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.880
Those are better, but they're a bit more tied to the evaluation data.

38
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.480
And they're just sort of heavier and more expensive to use.

39
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.360
So we have some of them and we will grow that list. Awesome.

40
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.600
And it really helps to know

41
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.280
which, skills really move the needle in more precise development with agents.

42
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:46.360
Amazing.

43
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:50.480
If you want to learn more, why don't you head over to tessl.io/registry,

44
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.280
where you can publish your own skills as well as learn and discover

45
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.040
about all the existing skills there today.
